1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,210
On the twenty-sixth of June 1945,

2
00:00:03,230 --> 00:00:05,623
about a hundred and fifty German prisoners

3
00:00:05,643 --> 00:00:08,381
of war were brought into the movie theater

4
00:00:08,401 --> 00:00:09,760
of Halloran General Hospital,

5
00:00:09,780 --> 00:00:11,484
on Staten Island, New York.

6
00:00:11,504 --> 00:00:14,587
The lights went down, and the Army ran a

7
00:00:14,607 --> 00:00:16,311
film made only weeks earlier,

8
00:00:16,331 --> 00:00:18,724
inside the camps the Allied armies had

9
00:00:18,744 --> 00:00:19,759
found in Germany.

10
00:00:19,779 --> 00:00:22,172
The New York Times reported what happened

11
00:00:22,192 --> 00:00:23,206
in the seats.

12
00:00:23,226 --> 00:00:25,964
A few men held handkerchiefs over their eyes.

13
00:00:25,984 --> 00:00:29,067
One sat with his head bowed and both hands

14
00:00:29,087 --> 00:00:32,190
pressed over his ears for most of the film.

15
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:36,610
The next day's headline did not speak of shame.

16
00:00:36,630 --> 00:00:38,697
It said the Germans were unmoved,

17
00:00:38,717 --> 00:00:41,131
and that the prisoners blamed the crimes

18
00:00:41,151 --> 00:00:43,218
on the men at the top.

19
00:00:43,238 --> 00:00:46,348
Yet that same summer, at a camp in North

20
00:00:46,368 --> 00:00:48,434
Carolina, about a thousand prisoners did

21
00:00:48,454 --> 00:00:50,173
something no one had ordered.

22
00:00:50,193 --> 00:00:52,280
They burned their own German uniforms.

23
00:00:53,675 --> 00:00:56,544
This is the documented story of the films

24
00:00:56,564 --> 00:00:57,266
the U.S.

25
00:00:57,286 --> 00:00:59,433
Army made German prisoners in America

26
00:00:59,453 --> 00:01:01,715
watch in 1945:

27
00:01:01,735 --> 00:01:04,612
a father and son who sat in the same camp,

28
00:01:04,632 --> 00:01:05,770
a thousand burned uniforms,

29
00:01:05,790 --> 00:01:07,798
and the survey that asked the prisoners,

30
00:01:07,818 --> 00:01:09,826
on their way home, whether they believed

31
00:01:09,846 --> 00:01:10,715
any of it.

32
00:01:12,300 --> 00:01:15,745
By the spring of 1945 there were nearly

33
00:01:15,765 --> 00:01:18,154
three hundred and eighty thousand German

34
00:01:18,174 --> 00:01:20,162
prisoners in the United States,

35
00:01:20,182 --> 00:01:22,170
spread over hundreds of camps.

36
00:01:22,190 --> 00:01:24,580
Many had been captured in Tunisia,

37
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:25,784
Italy and France.

38
00:01:25,804 --> 00:01:28,194
They picked cotton, apples and corn,

39
00:01:28,214 --> 00:01:30,603
and harvested wheat for American farmers.

40
00:01:30,623 --> 00:01:33,415
In the evenings, many still changed back

41
00:01:33,435 --> 00:01:35,021
into their German uniforms.

42
00:01:35,041 --> 00:01:37,832
In some camps, hard-line Nazis ran the

43
00:01:37,852 --> 00:01:39,860
barracks like a little Germany.

44
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:43,864
Gerhard Hennes was one of those prisoners.

45
00:01:43,884 --> 00:01:46,520
He was a young officer from Koblenz,

46
00:01:46,540 --> 00:01:49,184
on the Rhine, who had served in Rommel's

47
00:01:49,204 --> 00:01:50,515
army in North Africa.

48
00:01:50,535 --> 00:01:53,020
He was captured in May 1943,

49
00:01:53,040 --> 00:01:55,373
in the last pocket to surrender in

50
00:01:55,393 --> 00:01:58,062
Tunisia, and was sent to an officers' camp

51
00:01:58,082 --> 00:01:59,070
at Crossville, Tennessee.

52
00:01:59,090 --> 00:02:01,759
Years later he admitted that in the camp,

53
00:02:01,779 --> 00:02:05,140
most of them still clung to their faith in Hitler.

54
00:02:06,425 --> 00:02:09,932
In 1944 a prisoner postcard reached him

55
00:02:09,952 --> 00:02:12,100
from a camp in Trinidad, Colorado.

56
00:02:12,120 --> 00:02:14,765
It was from Major Friedrich Hennes,

57
00:02:14,785 --> 00:02:17,224
his father, who had been captured in France.

58
00:02:17,244 --> 00:02:19,068
His father asked for a transfer,

59
00:02:19,088 --> 00:02:21,834
paid for his own train ticket and for the

60
00:02:21,854 --> 00:02:23,986
ticket of the guard who watched him,

61
00:02:24,006 --> 00:02:26,138
and arrived in Crossville in time for

62
00:02:26,158 --> 00:02:28,245
Christmas 1944.

63
00:02:28,265 --> 00:02:30,965
They were the only father and son in the camp.

64
00:02:32,650 --> 00:02:34,783
In Washington, officials worried about

65
00:02:34,803 --> 00:02:38,229
sending these men home exactly as they had

66
00:02:38,249 --> 00:02:38,659
arrived.

67
00:02:38,679 --> 00:02:42,649
In 1944 the War Department agreed to a

68
00:02:42,669 --> 00:02:44,848
secret program to change their minds.

69
00:02:44,868 --> 00:02:47,047
Teaching politics to prisoners looked like

70
00:02:47,067 --> 00:02:49,246
a breach of the Geneva Convention,

71
00:02:49,266 --> 00:02:51,812
so the program borrowed a harmless line

72
00:02:51,832 --> 00:02:54,011
from Article Seventeen, and took a

73
00:02:54,031 --> 00:02:54,744
harmless name:

74
00:02:54,764 --> 00:02:56,230
the Intellectual Diversion Program.

75
00:02:57,525 --> 00:02:59,561
Its headquarters, nicknamed the Idea

76
00:02:59,581 --> 00:03:02,872
Factory, opened in October 1944 at a

77
00:03:02,892 --> 00:03:05,291
former Civilian Conservation Corps camp in

78
00:03:05,311 --> 00:03:08,517
upstate New York, and later moved to Fort

79
00:03:08,537 --> 00:03:09,727
Kearney, Rhode Island.

80
00:03:09,747 --> 00:03:11,743
About eighty-five German prisoners worked

81
00:03:11,763 --> 00:03:14,566
there, writers and teachers chosen as anti-Nazis.

82
00:03:14,586 --> 00:03:17,792
They printed books that had been banned in

83
00:03:17,812 --> 00:03:20,854
Germany, and from March 1945 they

84
00:03:20,874 --> 00:03:23,311
published a newspaper for the camps,

85
00:03:23,331 --> 00:03:24,540
called The Call.

86
00:03:24,560 --> 00:03:26,587
Hard-line Nazis called it Jewish

87
00:03:26,607 --> 00:03:28,245
propaganda and burned copies.

88
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:32,940
Films were part of the plan from the start.

89
00:03:32,960 --> 00:03:35,553
Nearly every camp had bought a sixteen

90
00:03:35,573 --> 00:03:37,420
millimeter projector, and prisoners paid

91
00:03:37,440 --> 00:03:38,913
fifteen cents a seat.

92
00:03:38,933 --> 00:03:41,900
The Idea Factory approved a list of a

93
00:03:41,920 --> 00:03:43,767
hundred and fifteen American features,

94
00:03:43,787 --> 00:03:46,753
among them Abe Lincoln in Illinois and The

95
00:03:46,773 --> 00:03:48,247
Adventures of Tom Sawyer.

96
00:03:48,267 --> 00:03:50,860
Gangster films and cowboy films were left

97
00:03:50,880 --> 00:03:52,000
off the list.

98
00:03:53,675 --> 00:03:56,295
Then, in the spring of 1945,

99
00:03:56,315 --> 00:03:59,361
the camps received footage of a different kind.

100
00:03:59,381 --> 00:04:01,278
Army cameramen had filmed the

101
00:04:01,298 --> 00:04:03,194
concentration camps the American and

102
00:04:03,214 --> 00:04:05,110
British armies entered in April.

103
00:04:05,130 --> 00:04:07,793
After Germany surrendered on the eighth of

104
00:04:07,813 --> 00:04:10,860
May, the films were sent to the prisoner

105
00:04:10,880 --> 00:04:12,010
camps in America.

106
00:04:12,030 --> 00:04:15,076
On the twelfth of May, the Des Moines

107
00:04:15,096 --> 00:04:17,375
Register reported that three hundred and

108
00:04:17,395 --> 00:04:19,292
forty-three thousand German prisoners in

109
00:04:19,312 --> 00:04:20,845
America were watching them.

110
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:23,305
Attendance was compulsory.

111
00:04:23,325 --> 00:04:26,572
In many camps, every man signed a register

112
00:04:26,592 --> 00:04:28,613
afterwards, and American officers wrote

113
00:04:28,633 --> 00:04:31,063
reports on how each audience behaved.

114
00:04:31,083 --> 00:04:34,330
With the films came a booklet in German,

115
00:04:34,350 --> 00:04:35,147
with photographs:

116
00:04:35,167 --> 00:04:37,597
German Concentration and Prisoner of War

117
00:04:37,617 --> 00:04:40,455
Camps, as the American and British Armies

118
00:04:40,475 --> 00:04:43,960
Found Them, April 1945.

119
00:04:43,980 --> 00:04:46,627
In some camps the prisoners had their own

120
00:04:46,647 --> 00:04:47,960
name for the screenings.

121
00:04:47,980 --> 00:04:49,980
They called them the bone films.

122
00:04:51,400 --> 00:04:53,754
The footage is still very hard to watch:

123
00:04:53,774 --> 00:04:55,832
survivors so thin they could barely stand,

124
00:04:55,852 --> 00:04:57,909
and the dead lying in the open.

125
00:04:57,929 --> 00:04:59,690
We will not show it here.

126
00:04:59,710 --> 00:05:01,768
What matters in this story is what

127
00:05:01,787 --> 00:05:04,142
happened in the dark, in the rows of

128
00:05:04,162 --> 00:05:06,516
seats, among men who had fought for the

129
00:05:06,536 --> 00:05:08,020
state that built those camps.

130
00:05:09,725 --> 00:05:12,242
At Crossville, the screening came in the

131
00:05:12,262 --> 00:05:15,142
spring, at the movie house outside the wire.

132
00:05:15,162 --> 00:05:17,094
Gerhard Hennes described the survivors'

133
00:05:17,114 --> 00:05:19,840
empty eyes, the mass graves and the ovens.

134
00:05:19,860 --> 00:05:22,585
We saw, he wrote, and stared in silence,

135
00:05:22,605 --> 00:05:24,987
struggling but unable to believe what we

136
00:05:25,007 --> 00:05:27,046
Germans had done to Jews, gypsies,

137
00:05:27,066 --> 00:05:29,125
prisoners of war and many others.

138
00:05:30,450 --> 00:05:32,868
He remembered the theater as dark and

139
00:05:32,888 --> 00:05:33,912
suddenly very hot.

140
00:05:33,932 --> 00:05:36,830
He admitted he had heard the name Dachau

141
00:05:36,850 --> 00:05:39,332
before the war, and had seen the pogrom of

142
00:05:39,352 --> 00:05:41,922
November 1938 in his own town.

143
00:05:41,942 --> 00:05:44,252
But he had no idea, he wrote,

144
00:05:44,272 --> 00:05:46,249
of the scale of the killing.

145
00:05:46,269 --> 00:05:48,246
That spring day, in his words,

146
00:05:48,266 --> 00:05:51,575
he turned from being a hero into being a villain.

147
00:05:51,595 --> 00:05:53,906
None of us in Crossville, he wrote,

148
00:05:53,926 --> 00:05:55,590
will ever forget that documentary.

149
00:05:57,100 --> 00:05:59,663
If you want more prisoner of war stories

150
00:05:59,683 --> 00:06:02,380
told from the documents, subscribe to WWII

151
00:06:02,400 --> 00:06:02,849
Frontlines.

152
00:06:02,869 --> 00:06:05,680
It helps us keep finding them.

153
00:06:07,300 --> 00:06:09,143
At Camp Butner, North Carolina,

154
00:06:09,163 --> 00:06:11,752
a camp built for about five thousand

155
00:06:11,772 --> 00:06:14,733
prisoners, the Army showed a film that ran

156
00:06:14,753 --> 00:06:15,478
twenty-five minutes.

157
00:06:15,498 --> 00:06:17,714
Afterwards, according to two histories of

158
00:06:17,734 --> 00:06:20,322
the prisoner camps, about a thousand men

159
00:06:20,342 --> 00:06:21,813
burned their German uniforms.

160
00:06:21,833 --> 00:06:23,303
The historian Judith Gansberg,

161
00:06:23,323 --> 00:06:25,912
who worked from the Army's own camp

162
00:06:25,932 --> 00:06:28,540
reports, wrote that they did it voluntarily.

163
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:32,665
It is the most dramatic reaction on

164
00:06:32,685 --> 00:06:35,735
record, but it was not the only one.

165
00:06:35,755 --> 00:06:37,653
At Eglin Field in Florida,

166
00:06:37,673 --> 00:06:40,338
prisoners took up a collection and gave

167
00:06:40,358 --> 00:06:42,256
two thousand, three hundred and

168
00:06:42,276 --> 00:06:44,558
seventy-one dollars to American war charities.

169
00:06:44,578 --> 00:06:47,244
In other camps, men collected money for

170
00:06:47,264 --> 00:06:48,011
the survivors.

171
00:06:48,031 --> 00:06:49,929
At Camp Shelby in Mississippi,

172
00:06:49,949 --> 00:06:52,144
a prisoner named Josef remembered the

173
00:06:52,164 --> 00:06:53,140
screening this way.

174
00:06:53,160 --> 00:06:55,796
It was hard for the Americans to believe,

175
00:06:55,816 --> 00:06:58,140
he said, but we really didn't know.

176
00:06:59,400 --> 00:07:01,167
In Iowa, at Camp Clarinda,

177
00:07:01,187 --> 00:07:03,310
the two hundred and twenty-five prisoners

178
00:07:03,330 --> 00:07:06,526
who saw the film wrote a letter to the

179
00:07:06,546 --> 00:07:08,670
camp commander, Lieutenant Colonel George Ball.

180
00:07:08,690 --> 00:07:10,457
They were veterans of Tunisia,

181
00:07:10,477 --> 00:07:11,529
Italy and France.

182
00:07:11,549 --> 00:07:13,315
The camp spokesman signed first,

183
00:07:13,335 --> 00:07:16,174
then every man who had seen the film.

184
00:07:16,194 --> 00:07:18,318
They called the crimes deplorable and

185
00:07:18,338 --> 00:07:21,533
wanted it on record that they had no part

186
00:07:21,553 --> 00:07:24,820
in what was done at Buchenwald.

187
00:07:24,840 --> 00:07:27,250
The letter went up the chain to the Army

188
00:07:27,270 --> 00:07:28,080
command in Omaha.

189
00:07:29,500 --> 00:07:31,537
At Camp Algona, in northern Iowa,

190
00:07:31,557 --> 00:07:33,936
the prisoners printed the story in their

191
00:07:33,956 --> 00:07:34,965
own camp newspaper.

192
00:07:34,985 --> 00:07:37,707
They wrote that they had turned away from

193
00:07:37,727 --> 00:07:39,764
the pictures with the utmost revulsion,

194
00:07:39,784 --> 00:07:42,163
and they declared their complete and total

195
00:07:42,183 --> 00:07:44,240
solidarity with the men at Clarinda.

196
00:07:45,950 --> 00:07:49,268
But many audiences did not react that way.

197
00:07:49,288 --> 00:07:51,355
At Halloran, the Times reported,

198
00:07:51,375 --> 00:07:54,275
most of the men stayed outwardly unmoved.

199
00:07:54,295 --> 00:07:56,252
At Fort DuPont, in Delaware,

200
00:07:56,272 --> 00:07:58,742
the prisoner who ran the projector told a

201
00:07:58,762 --> 00:08:00,609
reporter what the men were saying.

202
00:08:00,629 --> 00:08:02,787
Some of the men think, he said,

203
00:08:02,807 --> 00:08:05,276
the bodies in the movie are Germans whom

204
00:08:05,296 --> 00:08:06,230
the Russians tortured.

205
00:08:07,525 --> 00:08:09,451
The Army had expected that.

206
00:08:09,471 --> 00:08:11,785
The prisoners had been raised on

207
00:08:11,805 --> 00:08:13,731
propaganda, and they expected propaganda

208
00:08:13,751 --> 00:08:14,898
from their captors.

209
00:08:14,918 --> 00:08:17,233
Gansberg noted that the films convinced

210
00:08:17,253 --> 00:08:19,957
most where they mixed the camps with

211
00:08:19,977 --> 00:08:21,903
familiar German streets and faces,

212
00:08:21,923 --> 00:08:25,425
scenes that could not have been staged in America.

213
00:08:27,225 --> 00:08:31,136
By the autumn of 1945 the war was over

214
00:08:31,156 --> 00:08:33,492
everywhere, and the prisoners knew they

215
00:08:33,512 --> 00:08:35,062
would be sent back.

216
00:08:35,082 --> 00:08:37,418
Rumors spread that cooperative men would

217
00:08:37,438 --> 00:08:38,596
go home first.

218
00:08:38,616 --> 00:08:40,167
Suddenly, democracy was popular.

219
00:08:40,187 --> 00:08:43,308
At Camp Butner, the camp where a thousand

220
00:08:43,328 --> 00:08:45,271
men had burned their uniforms,

221
00:08:45,291 --> 00:08:47,627
the prisoners voted ninety-five percent in

222
00:08:47,647 --> 00:08:48,825
favor of democracy.

223
00:08:50,250 --> 00:08:52,849
The prisoner editors of The Call did not

224
00:08:52,869 --> 00:08:53,504
believe it.

225
00:08:53,524 --> 00:08:56,123
In an article written for the fifteenth of

226
00:08:56,143 --> 00:08:59,570
November 1945, and cut from the paper at

227
00:08:59,590 --> 00:09:01,403
the last moment, they wrote:

228
00:09:01,423 --> 00:09:03,603
Ninety-five percent, it sounds like an

229
00:09:03,623 --> 00:09:05,070
old, well-known fairy tale.

230
00:09:05,090 --> 00:09:08,210
As you know, Goebbels never allowed lower figures.

231
00:09:09,550 --> 00:09:11,773
The Army wanted to know too.

232
00:09:11,793 --> 00:09:13,269
Before the prisoners sailed,

233
00:09:13,289 --> 00:09:15,886
it gave an anonymous questionnaire to more

234
00:09:15,906 --> 00:09:18,504
than twenty-two thousand of them at the

235
00:09:18,524 --> 00:09:19,251
embarkation camps.

236
00:09:19,271 --> 00:09:22,243
Most gave the answers their captors hoped for.

237
00:09:22,263 --> 00:09:24,860
But only thirty percent accepted as true

238
00:09:24,880 --> 00:09:27,103
the reports of the concentration camps.

239
00:09:27,123 --> 00:09:29,721
The rest, the historian Ron Robin wrote,

240
00:09:29,741 --> 00:09:32,338
dismissed the films, the pictures and the

241
00:09:32,358 --> 00:09:33,834
booklets as mere propaganda.

242
00:09:33,854 --> 00:09:36,825
More than half still placed at least part

243
00:09:36,845 --> 00:09:40,210
of the blame for Germany's troubles on the Jews.

244
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:44,774
Many of the prisoners who sailed from

245
00:09:44,794 --> 00:09:46,770
America did not go home.

246
00:09:46,790 --> 00:09:49,563
They were handed over to Britain and

247
00:09:49,583 --> 00:09:50,761
France as laborers.

248
00:09:50,781 --> 00:09:52,974
Gerhard Hennes and his father left

249
00:09:52,994 --> 00:09:56,260
Crossville in December 1945.

250
00:09:56,280 --> 00:09:58,518
Before he went, Gerhard hid two medals in

251
00:09:58,538 --> 00:10:00,212
the lining of his toilet bag,

252
00:10:00,232 --> 00:10:02,471
and buried the rest of his medals and

253
00:10:02,491 --> 00:10:03,620
ribbons under his barrack.

254
00:10:04,825 --> 00:10:06,705
They were taken to a camp at Attichy,

255
00:10:06,725 --> 00:10:08,899
in France, where they went hungry.

256
00:10:08,919 --> 00:10:12,189
Each of them fell to about a hundred and

257
00:10:12,209 --> 00:10:12,920
ten pounds.

258
00:10:12,940 --> 00:10:15,479
They were released on the thirtieth of

259
00:10:15,499 --> 00:10:19,083
January 1946, thirteen years to the day

260
00:10:19,103 --> 00:10:21,068
after Hitler came to power.

261
00:10:21,088 --> 00:10:24,633
In 1948, the French occupation authorities

262
00:10:24,653 --> 00:10:26,840
removed Friedrich Hennes from his pulpit

263
00:10:26,860 --> 00:10:29,298
in Koblenz, because he had gone along with

264
00:10:29,318 --> 00:10:30,118
the Nazi Party.

265
00:10:31,550 --> 00:10:33,882
Gerhard went to work for Church World

266
00:10:33,902 --> 00:10:35,898
Service, a relief agency for refugees,

267
00:10:35,918 --> 00:10:39,276
and in 1958 he became an American citizen.

268
00:10:39,296 --> 00:10:41,878
He wrote that he had never fully recovered

269
00:10:41,898 --> 00:10:43,829
from the shock of that hot,

270
00:10:43,849 --> 00:10:45,150
dark theater in Crossville.

271
00:10:46,825 --> 00:10:49,074
If someone in your family was a prisoner

272
00:10:49,094 --> 00:10:51,626
of war in America, or worked in one of

273
00:10:51,646 --> 00:10:53,327
